## <a name="code-analysis"></a>Code analysis

The test method `PUT_CountContract` is a test method using the PexAssumeUnderTest Attribute and checks if the count value of the ArrayList is either 0 or greater than or equal to one.

The `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);` statement is a common practice in FLS (Field Level Security) tests. It can help to reduce some false positives, as it assumes an initial condition before the action.

`PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);` allows to observe the value of `New_Ret` in the Visual Studio Performance Explorer. 

Finally, the test checks if `0 <= New_Ret` is true. If this condition is met, the test passes; otherwise, it fails, showing the `New_Ret` value and an associated message to describe the error being detected by the test.

Just like most of the generated tests via Pex, this one proves to be a simple and robust test case that does not take edge conditions into account. However, we do encourage the use of Pex for any methods or classes that may have edge cases in production code. Edge cases are especially useful when code doesn’t have explicit branches (paths), as Pex can generate random input to cover all those paths. 

While it's important to note that Pex tests are designed to be thorough and can help to uncover possible problems, they are not exhaustive. Each method and class should be thoroughly unit tested and any method or class that interacts with other systems or has critical paths should be weighed by the system architects to decide on the level of testing required.

## <a name="conclusion"></a>Conclusions

Performance testing with Pex and observing method results can help you to improve your testing process by ensuring that the tests are not only thorough but also effective. Additionally, it is crucial to take into account the system's architecture and security requirements when designing the tests. Pex doesn't generate random inputs, providing control over the test execution and possible security issues. It’s recommended to combine Pex with other testing practices and automation frameworks to create comprehensive test suites. Happy testing and keep the code running smoothly.


>[Further reading](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/visualstudio/test/code-analyzer-settings-in-a-test-settings-file?view=vs-2019)

