## <a name="using-pexall-attribute-to-verify-all-possible-execution-paths-in-your-test"></a>使用 PexAll 属性验证测试中的所有可能执行路径

以下是使用 PexAll 属性验证所有可能执行路径的代码片段：

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethodUnderTest]
        [PexAll]
        public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int New_Ret = arrList.Count;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);

	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ count value of array is either 0 or greater than or equal to one 
	    try
            {
		Debug.Assert(New_Ret == 0 || New_Ret >= 1);
	    }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
		throw new Fail("Failure with the array list count value : " + ex.Message);
	    }
        }
    }
}
```

PexAll 属性会自动为测试中的每个可能的输入值执行属性方法。它会为方法中的每种输入数据类型找出所有可能的组合并进行测试。所以代码的所有可能路径都会被执行，验证所有可能的情况。


## <a name="limit-the-number-of-test-iterations-with-the-pexbasicloopsattribute"></a>使用 PexBasicLoops 属性限制测试迭代次数

PexBasicLoops 属性允许您定义测试运行中的迭代次数上限。

例如，可以限制以下测试迭代次数为 10000 次：

```csharp
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexBasicLoops(10000)]
public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    //...   
}
```

如果生成器生成的测试用例超过了指定的迭代次数，则会跳过该测试用例。


## <a name="limit-the-testing-time-with-the-pextimeoutattribute"></a>使用 PexTimeout 属性限制测试执行时间

可以使用 PexTimeout 属性在自动化测试执行中设置一个超时时间。如果在指定的超时时间内无法完成测试，则执行将取消。

例如，可以将一个测试函数的执行超时时间设置为 2 秒：

```csharp
[PexMethodUnderTest]
[PexTimeout(2000)]
public void PUT_CountContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList)
{
    //...   
}
```

如果函数超过了 2 秒，测试执行将被取消。


## <a name="customize-test-by-enforcing-preconditions-and-postconditions-with-pexcustombackingfieldattribute"></a>使用 PexCustomBackingField 属性强制执行前置条件和后置条件

如果要对数据结构进行自动化测试，通常需要对数据结构执行一些预定义的操作，例如初始化、操作和清理。

前置条件是某个操作执行前的一个事实陈述。后置条件是某个操作执行后的一些事实陈述。

例如，对于 ArrayList 对象的测试，可以在其构造函数中执行预定义的操作，然后测试其计数的结果。

```csharp
[PexMethodUnderTest]
public void PUT_CountContract()
{
    //Arrange
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList(); // Constructor
--------------------	    Debug.Assert((arrList.Count >= 0));

        }
    } }
```

#### Test Method: PUT_ItemContract

This test is similar to IndexerContract, but in this case, we're testing the get and set accessors of the collection's items.

```csharp

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_ItemContract(
            [PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, 
            [PexFromFile(typeof(int))]int index, 
            [PexFromFile(typeof(object))]object value)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(arrList != null && arrList.Count > index);

            object Original_Ret = arrList[index];

            arrList[index] = value;

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$Original_Ret", Original_Ret);

            // Assert the value was set correctly in the list
            Assert.AreEqual(value, arrList[index]);

            // Reset the value back to the original value
            arrList[index] = Original_Ret;
        }
    }
}
```

This contract tests the operation of getting and setting values using the indexer of the collection. It assumes that the provided ArrayList instance is not null and contains at least one element.

Please note that the test runner is not capable of making any assumptions about the contents of your list before testing it. The contract tests are meant to be a guideline to guide your tests. If your test needs to consider specific elements in your ArrayList instance, you would need to expand these tests accordingly.

**Testing the add and remove methods**

These methods could also be tested in a similar way. The key would be to add an element to a collection, verify that the element exists in the collection, then remove the element and verify it no longer exists.

To write more robust tests, you would generally want to test these basic operations with a variety of possible inputs, as well as possible boundary and edge cases. It's very possible to write a set of rigorous unit tests for your ArrayList implementation that could catch a lot of basic mistakes and common pitfalls.

That being said, this is merely a basic starting point and there is a lot more to testing that could be done. I hope you find these tests useful and expand on them with more specifics tailored to your application's needs. Good luck testing your code with PexArrayList.TestFramework.

